  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B017 as my preferred achievement. Optical lattice clocks turn precise control of atoms and light into a reproducible frequency standard, enabling tests of fundamental physics as well as exceptional timekeeping. Katori’s concept and Ye’s experimental advances form a coherent, complementary contribution, and the method has a long record of independent realization.",
    "case_against": "The field’s progress involved many groups, so credit to two people must be tied to their foundational contributions rather than later improvements across the clock community. Some of the most far-reaching applications are still developing.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson supports the scientific importance and maturity of optical lattice clocks, while noting that the early division of credit and later enabling advances need careful historical assessment.",
        "response": "I agree that the historical boundary deserves scrutiny. That concern qualifies the attribution, but does not erase the distinct conceptual and experimental contributions of Katori and Ye or the achievement’s demonstrated maturity."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that Kane and Mele’s theory and Molenkamp’s experimental realization form a coherent, mature contribution that established a major new phase of matter.",
        "response": "The theory–experiment pairing and lasting conceptual impact make B026 a strong alternative. Still, its broad topological-insulator scope includes later contributions that complicate the prize boundary; B017 offers a more directly measurable advance with a sustained record of precision and reproducibility."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The relative contributions of early clock groups and subsequent enabling advances require careful historical reconstruction."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
